From 52c2171d7094971db2d5297c28bbe3ae5f9dc829 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: desocketed <desocketed@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 17:38:22 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] vk_memory: the write-back compare runs on a small thread pool
 (NX2NAVI_DIFF_THREADS)

A submission's written ranges (162 of them, 66 MiB, per Hyrule Field frame on Cal's 2026-11-09 Tracy trace) were
compared page by page one after the other on the completion thread, 5.4 ms per frame at 12 GB/s: memory-bound, and
on the path to `done`, so on every real fence wait.

vk_gpu_written_many takes the whole list from the draw recorder's completion callback (cb_done). It resolves the
ranges with a shadow under pool_lock (shared, held across the call), lays them end to end and cuts that byte span into
one slice per worker, slice edges rounded up to a page edge by address so no page is compared twice and none is
missed. The completion thread takes slice 0, a persistent pool of N-1 threads ("vk wb diff k", started on the first
call, woken by a generation counter and a condition variable, no per-frame thread creation) the rest; each lists the
changed runs of its slice in order. Then, under shadow_lock, the lists are walked in worker order: that is address
order within a range and list order across ranges, so the copies (shadow_put) land exactly as the sequential path's
did; a run cut at a slice edge is two adjacent copies. The pending-write entries are dropped and the ranges without a
shadow are stamped (vk_guest_written) as before.

NX2NAVI_DIFF_THREADS=n, default 4, 1 = the old loop over vk_gpu_written (the single-range path stays for
vk_exec_xfer and the probes). Expected: the compare drops to ~1.5-2 ms, which shortens `done` for the fence waits and
the LOD guard, and the frame by what the submit finish wait still spends in it (NX2NAVI_GPU_WAIT).
---
